window.QUIZ_BANK['testng'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['testng']||[]).concat([
{id:2181,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In TestNG, when a subclass does not override a method annotated with @BeforeMethod in a superclass, what happens?',options:['The @BeforeMethod is ignored in the subclass','The @BeforeMethod from the superclass is inherited and executed before each test in the subclass','The subclass must explicitly call super to trigger it','TestNG throws a configuration error'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG supports annotation inheritance. If a parent class declares @BeforeMethod, all subclasses automatically inherit that setup behaviour. The annotated method runs before every @Test method in the subclass without any explicit super() call.'},
{id:2182,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which TestNG annotation attribute prevents an inherited @BeforeMethod from executing in a subclass?',options:['@BeforeMethod(inheritGroups=false)','@BeforeMethod(alwaysRun=false)','There is no built-in attribute; you must override and leave the method empty','@BeforeMethod(enabled=false) on the subclass method'],answer:2,explanation:'TestNG has no attribute to suppress an inherited configuration method. The standard workaround is to override the method in the subclass with an empty body, effectively disabling the inherited logic while satisfying the inheritance contract.'},
{id:2183,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When a TestNG test class inherits from an abstract base class, in which order do configuration methods execute?',options:['Subclass @BeforeClass first, then superclass @BeforeClass','Superclass @BeforeClass first, then subclass @BeforeClass','They run in parallel','Only the subclass configuration runs'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG follows JUnit-like inheritance semantics: superclass configuration methods run before subclass ones. So @BeforeClass in the parent executes first, then @BeforeClass in the child, mirroring object construction order.'},
{id:2184,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'An abstract base class declares @BeforeMethod setup() and a concrete subclass also declares @BeforeMethod setup(). What is the execution result?',options:['Only the subclass setup() runs because it overrides the parent','Both run: superclass setup() first, then subclass setup()','Both run: subclass setup() first, then superclass setup()','TestNG throws a duplicate method exception'],answer:0,explanation:'When a subclass overrides an inherited @BeforeMethod with the same signature, the override takes effect and only the subclass version executes. The parent version is hidden, not called, unless the subclass explicitly calls super.setup().'},
{id:2185,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the recommended pattern for sharing common @BeforeMethod logic while allowing subclasses to add their own setup in TestNG?',options:['Use @BeforeMethod(alwaysRun=true) in the parent only','Declare a final @BeforeMethod in the parent that calls an overridable doSetup() hook method','Copy-paste setup code into every subclass','Use @Factory to create subclass instances'],answer:1,explanation:'The Template Method design pattern works well here. The parent declares a final @BeforeMethod that calls an overridable hook like doSetup(). Subclasses override doSetup() to add specific behaviour without disrupting the parent lifecycle.'},
{id:2186,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Which static import statement is required to use assertEquals without a class prefix in a TestNG test?',options:['import static org.testng.Assert.*;','import static org.junit.Assert.*;','import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.*;','import org.testng.Assert;'],answer:0,explanation:'TestNG assertion methods reside in org.testng.Assert. Using import static org.testng.Assert.* brings all assertion methods (assertEquals, assertTrue, assertNull, etc.) into scope so they can be called without the Assert. prefix.'},
{id:2187,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the key behavioural difference between org.testng.Assert.assertEquals and AssertJ\'s assertThat(...).isEqualTo() when used inside TestNG tests?',options:['TestNG Assert throws TestException; AssertJ throws AssertionError','TestNG Assert halts test execution on failure; AssertJ soft-assertions can continue','There is no difference','AssertJ cannot be used with TestNG'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG Assert methods throw AssertionError immediately, stopping the test. AssertJ\'s SoftAssertions (or assertSoftly) collect all failures and report them together at the end, enabling multiple assertions per test without early exit.'},
{id:2188,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In TestNG, how do you perform multiple assertions and always report all failures rather than stopping at the first one?',options:['Use SoftAssert: instantiate SoftAssert, call assertAll() at the end','Use Assert.assertAll() directly','Enable softAssertions=true in testng.xml','Use @Test(alwaysRun=true)'],answer:0,explanation:'TestNG provides the org.testng.asserts.SoftAssert class. You create an instance, call assertion methods on it, and finally call softAssert.assertAll(). This collects all failures and throws a combined AssertionError only when assertAll() is reached.'},
{id:2189,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What pitfall arises when a SoftAssert instance is shared across multiple @Test methods in the same class?',options:['No pitfall; SoftAssert is thread-safe','Failures from one test bleed into another because the instance accumulates failures across calls','SoftAssert cannot be reused; it throws an exception after the first assertAll()','The second test always passes'],answer:1,explanation:'SoftAssert is not automatically reset between tests. If the instance is a class field and assertAll() is not called (or the previous test fails before calling it), accumulated failures persist and corrupt subsequent tests. Best practice is to create a new SoftAssert per test.'},
{id:2190,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which TestNG Reporter method is used to log messages that appear in the HTML test report?',options:['System.out.println','Reporter.log(String message)','Logger.info(message)','TestNG.log(message)'],answer:1,explanation:'org.testng.Reporter.log(String) writes messages to the TestNG HTML report. Unlike System.out, these messages are captured per test and displayed alongside pass/fail status in the emailable-report.html and index.html output files.'},
{id:2191,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you assert that a specific string was written to standard output during a TestNG test?',options:['Use Reporter.assertOutput()','Redirect System.out to a ByteArrayOutputStream before the test and inspect it afterward','TestNG captures stdout automatically; use getOutput()','Use @Test(captureOutput=true)'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG has no built-in stdout assertion API. The standard approach is to replace System.out with a custom PrintStream backed by a ByteArrayOutputStream, run the code under test, then inspect the captured bytes for the expected string.'},
{id:2192,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the correct way to restore System.out after capturing it in a TestNG test?',options:['Call System.resetOut()','Store the original PrintStream before replacing it and reassign it in @AfterMethod','TestNG restores it automatically','Use @Test(restoreStreams=true)'],answer:1,explanation:'Java has no System.resetOut(). The robust pattern is to save the original stream with PrintStream original = System.out, replace it in @BeforeMethod, run assertions, then call System.setOut(original) in an @AfterMethod to guarantee restoration even on test failure.'},
{id:2193,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is WireMock and how does it integrate with TestNG?',options:['WireMock is a TestNG plugin for mocking databases','WireMock is an HTTP server simulator that stubs and verifies HTTP calls; it integrates with TestNG via @BeforeClass lifecycle methods','WireMock is a built-in TestNG feature','WireMock only works with JUnit'],answer:1,explanation:'WireMock (com.github.tomakehurst:wiremock) starts an embedded HTTP server that you configure to return stubbed responses. In TestNG you start the server in @BeforeClass, configure stubs per test or method, and shut it down in @AfterClass.'},
{id:2194,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which WireMock method registers a stub that returns HTTP 200 with a JSON body?',options:['WireMock.mock(200, jsonBody)','stubFor(get(urlEqualTo("/api")).willReturn(aResponse().withStatus(200).withBody(json)))','WireMock.stub("/api", 200, json)','register(new Stub("/api", 200, json))'],answer:1,explanation:'WireMock\'s fluent DSL uses WireMock.stubFor() (static import from com.github.tomakehurst.wiremock.client.WireMock). You chain get(), post(), etc., then willReturn(aResponse()) to specify status, headers, and body.'},
{id:2195,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you verify that a specific HTTP endpoint was called exactly once during a TestNG test using WireMock?',options:['WireMock.assertCalled(1, "/api")','verify(1, getRequestedFor(urlEqualTo("/api")))','assertEquals(1, WireMock.getCallCount("/api"))','WireMock automatically fails if the endpoint is not called'],answer:1,explanation:'WireMock\'s verify() method asserts request count: verify(exactly(1), getRequestedFor(urlEqualTo("/path"))). This is called after the code under test runs. WireMock.verify() throws a VerificationException if the assertion is not met.'},
{id:2196,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In a TestNG abstract base test class, what is the purpose of declaring @BeforeSuite in the abstract class?',options:['It runs before every test method','It runs once before any test in the entire suite, providing one-time global setup shared by all subclasses','It marks the class as abstract','It prevents subclasses from overriding suite setup'],answer:1,explanation:'@BeforeSuite runs exactly once per suite execution. Placing it in an abstract base class means all concrete subclasses benefit from the global setup (e.g., starting a database or loading configuration) without duplicating code.'},
{id:2197,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happens if both an abstract superclass and its concrete subclass declare @BeforeSuite methods in TestNG?',options:['Only the first one encountered runs','Both run; the superclass @BeforeSuite executes first','TestNG throws a configuration conflict error','Only the subclass @BeforeSuite runs'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG executes both @BeforeSuite methods. Inheritance rules apply: the superclass annotated method runs before the subclass one. This allows layered initialisation where the parent sets up infrastructure and the child adds application-specific configuration.'},
{id:2198,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Page Object Model (POM) pattern with TestNG, what role does a base page class typically serve?',options:['It contains all @Test methods','It holds the WebDriver instance and common navigation utilities inherited by all page objects','It replaces testng.xml','It is used only for reporting'],answer:1,explanation:'A BasePage class centralises the WebDriver reference and shared methods like waitForElement(), navigate(), and getTitle(). All page object classes extend BasePage, ensuring consistent driver access and reuse of common UI interactions across the test suite.'},
{id:2199,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How is WebDriver typically passed to page objects in a TestNG POM framework?',options:['Through a static global variable','Via constructor injection into page objects from the test class','TestNG injects it automatically via @Inject','Via a ThreadLocal accessed from a DriverFactory utility'],answer:3,explanation:'In thread-safe POM frameworks, a DriverFactory class stores the WebDriver in a ThreadLocal<WebDriver>. This allows parallel test execution where each thread has its own driver instance, preventing race conditions when multiple tests run simultaneously.'},
{id:2200,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Why is ThreadLocal<WebDriver> preferred over a static WebDriver field in parallel TestNG POM frameworks?',options:['ThreadLocal is faster than static','ThreadLocal gives each thread its own independent copy, preventing driver state from being shared across parallel tests','Static fields are not allowed in TestNG','ThreadLocal is required by the Selenium specification'],answer:1,explanation:'A static WebDriver field is shared across all threads, causing tests to interfere with each other during parallel execution. ThreadLocal ensures each thread gets its own WebDriver instance, so parallel tests operate on independent browser sessions without cross-contamination.'},
{id:2201,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What command-line argument runs only tests belonging to the "smoke" group when using the TestNG CLI?',options:['-group smoke','-groups smoke','-run smoke','-testgroup smoke'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG\'s command-line runner accepts -groups followed by a comma-separated list of group names. For example: java org.testng.TestNG testng.xml -groups smoke. Only tests annotated with @Test(groups="smoke") or groups matching the expression will be executed.'},
{id:2202,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you run multiple groups from the command line in TestNG?',options:['-groups smoke -groups regression','-groups "smoke,regression"','-groups smoke|regression','-group smoke,regression'],answer:1,explanation:'Multiple groups are passed as a single comma-separated string: -groups "smoke,regression". TestNG treats this as a union, running tests belonging to either group. Using a space-separated or piped list is not the correct syntax for the TestNG CLI runner.'},
{id:2203,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which testng.xml element allows you to include and exclude groups for a suite?',options:['<filter>','<groups> with <run> containing <include> and <exclude>','<group-filter>','<test groups="smoke">'],answer:1,explanation:'The <groups> element inside <suite> or <test> contains a <run> block with <include name="groupName"/> and <exclude name="groupName"/> children. This declarative approach in testng.xml is the primary way to filter groups without command-line arguments.'},
{id:2204,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When -groups is specified on the command line alongside a testng.xml that also specifies groups, which takes precedence in TestNG?',options:['testng.xml always wins','Command-line -groups override the testng.xml group configuration','They are merged','TestNG throws a conflict error'],answer:1,explanation:'Command-line group filters override those defined in testng.xml. This allows CI pipelines to dynamically select test groups without modifying the XML configuration file, enabling flexible test scoping across different pipeline stages.'},
{id:2205,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What Gradle plugin provides first-class TestNG support including the useTestNG() configuration block?',options:['testng-gradle-plugin','The built-in Gradle java plugin via test { useTestNG() }','gradle-testng-plugin','org.gradle.testng'],answer:1,explanation:'Gradle\'s built-in java plugin includes a Test task that supports TestNG natively. You configure it by calling useTestNG() inside the test block and optionally passing a TestNGOptions closure to set suite XML files, groups, and listeners.'},
{id:2206,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you specify a custom testng.xml suite file in a Gradle build?',options:['testng { suiteFile = "testng.xml" }','test { useTestNG { suites("testng.xml") } }','gradle testng --suite testng.xml','apply plugin: "testng"; suite "testng.xml"'],answer:1,explanation:'In Gradle\'s test block, useTestNG() accepts a closure of TestNGOptions. Calling suites("path/to/testng.xml") tells Gradle to run TestNG using that suite file rather than auto-discovering tests. Multiple files can be listed.'},
{id:2207,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Gradle testSets plugin provide beyond the built-in test task?',options:['It replaces TestNG with its own runner','It allows defining multiple named test source sets (e.g., integrationTest, smokeTest) each with independent dependencies and tasks','It adds TestNG support to Gradle','It generates testng.xml automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'The com.energizedwork.webdriver-binaries or unbroken-dome testSets plugin (org.unbroken-dome.test-sets) allows declaring additional test sets beyond the standard "test". Each set gets its own source directory, configuration, and Gradle task, useful for separating unit, integration, and smoke tests.'},
{id:2208,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you pass TestNG groups to the Gradle test task at build time?',options:['gradle test -Pgroups=smoke','test { useTestNG { includeGroups "smoke" } } or via -PincludeGroups=smoke with dynamic configuration','gradle test --groups smoke','testng { groups = ["smoke"] }'],answer:1,explanation:'The TestNGOptions object exposes includeGroups and excludeGroups properties. You can hardcode them or read a project property dynamically: includeGroups project.findProperty("groups")?.split(",") ?: []. This allows CI commands like gradle test -Pgroups=smoke.'},
{id:2209,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Where does TestNG write its default HTML test reports after a test run?',options:['target/reports','test-output/ directory in the project root','build/reports/tests','src/test/results'],answer:1,explanation:'By default TestNG generates reports in the test-output directory relative to the working directory. The directory contains index.html, emailable-report.html, testng-results.xml, and group/method-level output for debugging failures.'},
{id:2210,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which file in the TestNG test-output directory is suitable for email distribution of test results?',options:['index.html','emailable-report.html','testng-results.xml','output.txt'],answer:1,explanation:'emailable-report.html is a single self-contained HTML file designed for email distribution. Unlike index.html which uses frames and multiple files, emailable-report.html is a standalone document summarising passed, failed, and skipped tests with method details.'},
{id:2211,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you analyse the root cause of a flaky TestNG test that occasionally fails?',options:['Delete the test','Enable verbose logging with -verbose 10, review test-output logs, and check for timing or state dependencies','Increase the timeout only','Add @Test(retryAnalyzer=...) without investigation'],answer:1,explanation:'Setting -verbose 10 (or verbosity in testng.xml) produces detailed execution logs. Reviewing the test-output directory for specific failure messages, stack traces, and timing data helps identify race conditions, shared state, and environmental dependencies causing flakiness.'},
{id:2212,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a RetryAnalyzer in TestNG and when should it be used?',options:['It re-runs the entire suite on any failure','It implements IRetryAnalyzer to retry a specific failing test up to a configured limit, used for genuinely flaky infrastructure tests','It is used to retry data providers','It automatically fixes failing tests'],answer:1,explanation:'IRetryAnalyzer.retry() returns true to re-run a failed test, incrementing an attempt counter. It should be used sparingly for tests that fail due to external instability (network timeouts, browser rendering). Overuse masks real defects and inflates run times.'},
{id:2213,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In TestNG, what exception type does Assert.fail() throw?',options:['java.lang.AssertionError','org.testng.TestException','java.lang.RuntimeException','org.testng.SkipException'],answer:0,explanation:'TestNG\'s Assert methods, including Assert.fail(), throw java.lang.AssertionError, which is a subclass of Error, not Exception. This is the same type thrown by JUnit assertions, making interoperability straightforward.'},
{id:2214,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What exception causes a TestNG test to be marked as SKIPPED rather than FAILED?',options:['java.lang.AssertionError','org.testng.SkipException','java.lang.Exception','org.testng.TestException'],answer:1,explanation:'Throwing org.testng.SkipException inside a test or configuration method signals TestNG to mark the test as SKIP instead of FAIL. This is useful for conditional skipping based on runtime conditions like missing environment variables or unavailable services.'},
{id:2215,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does TestNG\'s exception hierarchy relate to standard Java exceptions in terms of test outcome classification?',options:['All exceptions cause FAIL; only AssertionError causes FAIL differently','AssertionError → FAIL; SkipException → SKIP; any other Throwable not declared in expectedExceptions → FAIL','Only RuntimeException causes FAIL','TestNG has its own exception hierarchy independent of Java'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG maps throwables to outcomes: AssertionError marks the test FAILED, SkipException marks it SKIPPED, and any unexpected Throwable not listed in @Test(expectedExceptions) also marks the test FAILED. Declared expected exceptions cause the test to PASS if thrown.'},
{id:2216,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you pass a system property to TestNG tests via Maven Surefire plugin?',options:['<systemProperties><property><name>env</name><value>staging</value></property></systemProperties> inside the Surefire plugin configuration','testng.xml <parameter name="env" value="staging"/>','java -Denv=staging only','@Test(systemProperty="env=staging")'],answer:0,explanation:'The Maven Surefire plugin\'s <systemProperties> or <systemPropertyVariables> configuration block sets JVM system properties for the test JVM. Tests then read them via System.getProperty("env"). This is separate from testng.xml parameters.'},
{id:2217,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the testng.xml equivalent of passing a system property to parameterise test configuration?',options:['<jvmarg value="-Dkey=value"/>','<parameter name="key" value="value"/> inside <suite> or <test>, then @Parameters({"key"}) in the test method','<property name="key" value="value"/>','<config key="value"/>'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG\'s <parameter> element in testng.xml passes values to @Parameters-annotated methods. The parameter name matches the annotation string and TestNG injects the value as a method argument. This is distinct from JVM system properties.'},
{id:2218,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can a TestNG test read the value of an environment variable?',options:['@Parameter("ENV_VAR")','System.getenv("ENV_VAR") in Java code inside the test','testng.xml <envvar name="ENV_VAR"/>','TestNG.getEnvironment("ENV_VAR")'],answer:1,explanation:'Java\'s System.getenv(String) reads OS-level environment variables. Unlike system properties, environment variables are set in the shell or CI pipeline configuration. TestNG has no special annotation for them; tests read them directly via the standard Java API.'},
{id:2219,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a common pattern for switching test environments (dev/staging/prod) in TestNG without changing testng.xml?',options:['Hardcode URLs per environment','Read an ENV or PROFILE environment variable in a @BeforeSuite method to load environment-specific properties files','Use a different testng.xml per environment','Environments cannot be switched dynamically'],answer:1,explanation:'A @BeforeSuite method reads System.getenv("TEST_ENV") or System.getProperty("env"), then loads the corresponding properties file (e.g., staging.properties). All tests reference a shared config object populated from that file, enabling environment switching via a single environment variable.'},
{id:2220,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is DataProvider chaining in TestNG?',options:['Running one DataProvider after another sequentially','Composing data from multiple DataProviders by having one DataProvider call another to combine or transform datasets','Having two @Test methods share the same DataProvider','Chaining is not possible in TestNG'],answer:1,explanation:'A DataProvider method can call another DataProvider and merge or transform its result. For example, a parameterised DataProvider might call a base data-loading method and add extra columns or filter rows. This promotes reuse of test data generation logic.'},
{id:2221,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can a TestNG @DataProvider return different data sets based on the test method being called?',options:['It cannot distinguish between test methods','The DataProvider receives a java.lang.reflect.Method parameter; inspect method.getName() to return method-specific data','Use separate DataProviders for every method','Pass the method name as a system property'],answer:1,explanation:'When a DataProvider method declares a java.lang.reflect.Method parameter (or ITestNGMethod), TestNG injects the calling test method. The provider can branch on method.getName() or annotations to return tailored datasets, enabling a single provider to serve multiple tests with distinct data.'},
{id:2222,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Can TestNG @DataProvider be parallelised independently of the test method\'s parallel setting?',options:['No; DataProvider parallelism follows the test method\'s parallel setting','Yes; @DataProvider(parallel=true) runs each data row invocation in a separate thread','DataProvider is always single-threaded','Only if the suite uses parallel="methods"'],answer:1,explanation:'@DataProvider(parallel=true) instructs TestNG to execute each parameterised invocation of the test method in a separate thread. This is independent of the suite-level parallel setting and dramatically reduces total run time for data-intensive tests.'},
{id:2223,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Can TestNG test classes contain nested static inner classes with @Test methods?',options:['No; TestNG only supports top-level classes','Yes; TestNG can discover and run static nested classes annotated appropriately or listed in testng.xml','Yes; but only if they implement ITest','Only with the @Nested annotation like JUnit 5'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG supports static nested classes as test classes. They must be listed explicitly in testng.xml using their canonical name (OuterClass$InnerClass) or discovered via class scanning. Instance inner classes are not supported because TestNG cannot instantiate them without an outer instance.'},
{id:2224,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a limitation of using non-static nested classes as TestNG test classes?',options:['They cannot use @BeforeMethod','TestNG cannot instantiate them without an enclosing outer class instance, so only static nested classes work reliably','They cannot use @DataProvider','They always run in parallel'],answer:1,explanation:'Non-static inner classes require an enclosing instance. TestNG\'s default instantiation mechanism creates test objects using the no-argument constructor, which cannot satisfy the enclosing instance requirement. Static nested classes avoid this issue because they have no enclosing instance dependency.'},
{id:2225,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a database cleanup strategy for TestNG integration tests that use a shared database?',options:['Delete the entire database before each test','Use @AfterMethod (or @AfterClass) with a rollback or DELETE to undo data changes, or use @Transactional rollback via Spring','Never clean up; use isolated schemas per test','Use a mock database always'],answer:1,explanation:'Common strategies include: transactional rollback (roll back the test transaction in @AfterMethod), explicit DELETE in @AfterMethod for specific tables, database snapshots, or embedded in-memory databases (H2) that reset on restart. The choice depends on whether real database behaviour is required.'},
{id:2226,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Why is transactional rollback in @AfterMethod sometimes insufficient for cleaning up database state in TestNG tests?',options:['Transactions always work; this is not a limitation','Tests that trigger operations in separate threads, async processes, or remote services commit outside the test transaction, leaving data that rollback cannot undo','TestNG does not support @AfterMethod with transactions','Rollback only works with Oracle'],answer:1,explanation:'Transactional rollback only undoes changes within the test\'s own transaction boundary. If the code under test spawns async tasks, calls separate microservices, or uses its own transaction, those commits happen outside the test transaction and persist after rollback.'},
{id:2227,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is JIRA Xray and how does it integrate with TestNG?',options:['Xray is a TestNG plugin for parallel execution','Xray is a JIRA test management app; it integrates with TestNG by consuming JUnit-format XML results or via its REST API to update test execution records in JIRA','Xray replaces TestNG reporting','Xray is only for manual tests'],answer:1,explanation:'Xray (by Xpand IT) is a JIRA app for test management. It can import TestNG results from testng-results.xml or JUnit-format XML via its REST API or Maven/Gradle plugin. Tests are linked to JIRA Test issues and execution results are stored in JIRA.'},
{id:2228,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which Xray annotation can be used on a TestNG test method to link it to a JIRA test issue?',options:['@XrayTest(key="PROJ-123")','@Test(testKey="PROJ-123")','@JiraTest("PROJ-123")','@Requirement("PROJ-123")'],answer:0,explanation:'The Xray Java client library provides the @XrayTest annotation with a key attribute corresponding to the JIRA issue key. When results are exported, Xray maps the annotated test method to the specified Test issue and updates its execution status.'},
{id:2229,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Zephyr Scale (formerly TM4J) in the context of TestNG?',options:['A Gradle plugin for TestNG','A JIRA test management add-on where TestNG results can be imported to update test cycles and executions','A TestNG assertion library','A code coverage tool'],answer:1,explanation:'Zephyr Scale (SmartBear, previously TM4J) is a JIRA test management tool. TestNG results (from testng-results.xml) can be imported via its REST API or CI plugins to update test cycles, executions, and traceability matrices within JIRA.'},
{id:2230,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you export TestNG results in JUnit XML format for compatibility with tools like Zephyr Scale?',options:['TestNG natively generates JUnit XML in test-output/','Add the JUnitReportReporter listener or configure Gradle/Maven to generate JUnit XML alongside TestNG XML','Use @Test(format="junit")','TestNG results are already JUnit-compatible'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG\'s default output is testng-results.xml, not JUnit format. To generate JUnit-compatible XML, add org.testng.reporters.JUnitReportReporter as a listener, or configure the Maven Surefire plugin with <reportsDirectory> pointing to surefire-reports, which naturally produces JUnit XML.'},
{id:2231,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which TestNG metric indicates the percentage of test methods that passed out of all executed methods?',options:['Coverage rate','Pass rate: (passed / (passed + failed + skipped)) * 100','Execution efficiency','Suite health score'],answer:1,explanation:'Pass rate is the most basic test run metric: passed tests divided by total executed tests. It excludes skipped tests from the denominator in some interpretations, or includes them in others. TestNG\'s HTML reports display these counts directly for each suite and test.'},
{id:2232,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What TestNG listener interface would you implement to collect custom metrics like average test duration?',options:['ITestListener — override onTestSuccess/onTestFailure/onTestStart to record timestamps','IReporter — only for final report generation','ISuiteListener — only for suite-level events','IAnnotationTransformer — for modifying annotations'],answer:0,explanation:'ITestListener.onTestStart records the start timestamp. onTestSuccess, onTestFailure, and onTestSkipped capture the end time. The difference gives per-test duration. Aggregated across all tests, you can compute average, min, max, and percentile durations for reporting.'},
{id:2233,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the IReporter interface in TestNG used for?',options:['Marking tests to be reported to JIRA','Implementing a custom final report generator called once after all tests complete; receives suite results for full customisation','Real-time test monitoring','Generating screenshots'],answer:1,explanation:'IReporter.generateReport(List<XmlSuite>, List<ISuite>, String outputDir) is invoked once after the entire suite finishes. You receive full result data and can generate any custom format: HTML, CSV, PDF, or push results to external systems like dashboards or test management tools.'},
{id:2234,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does parallel="instances" mean in a TestNG testng.xml configuration?',options:['One thread per test method','One thread per @Test class instance; each @Factory-created instance runs in its own thread','One thread per suite','Tests run sequentially'],answer:1,explanation:'parallel="instances" (introduced in TestNG 6.x) creates a separate thread for each test class instance. This is especially powerful with @Factory, where many instances of the same class are created with different constructor parameters and each runs fully parallel.'},
{id:2235,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between parallel="methods" and parallel="instances" in TestNG?',options:['They are identical','parallel="methods" gives each test method its own thread regardless of instance; parallel="instances" gives each class instance its own thread, keeping same-instance methods sequential relative to each other','parallel="instances" is faster in all cases','parallel="methods" is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'With parallel="methods", any method can be assigned any thread, which can cause instance-level race conditions if tests in the same instance share mutable state. parallel="instances" ensures all methods of one instance run in the same thread, preventing intra-instance concurrency issues while still parallelising across instances.'},
{id:2236,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @Factory in TestNG and how does it enable parallel instance-level testing?',options:['@Factory marks a test method to run with a factory method pattern','@Factory annotates a method that returns Object[] of test class instances with different parameters; combined with parallel="instances", each instance runs in parallel','@Factory creates mock objects','@Factory is equivalent to @DataProvider'],answer:1,explanation:'@Factory annotates a method returning Object[] of test instances (typically constructed with different parameters). TestNG runs all @Test methods on each instance. With parallel="instances", each instance gets its own thread, allowing massive parallelisation of parameterised test scenarios.'},
{id:2237,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you limit the number of threads used by TestNG parallel execution?',options:['parallel="methods" thread-count="4" in <suite>','Set thread-count attribute on the <suite> element: <suite parallel="methods" thread-count="8">','Use @Test(maxThreads=4)','Java system property -Dtestng.threads=8'],answer:1,explanation:'The thread-count attribute on the <suite> element caps the thread pool size. For example, <suite parallel="methods" thread-count="8"> limits TestNG to 8 concurrent threads. Without this, TestNG may create too many threads, overwhelming available CPU or external resources.'},
{id:2238,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What technique reduces total TestNG test run time without changing individual test logic?',options:['Removing assertions','Parallelising independent tests and grouping slow integration tests to run separately from fast unit tests','Increasing timeouts','Using a single-threaded suite'],answer:1,explanation:'Parallel execution of independent tests is the primary time-reduction strategy. Additionally, separating unit tests (milliseconds) from integration tests (seconds) into different suites allows fast feedback during development while integration tests run only in CI pipelines.'},
{id:2239,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @Test(timeOut=5000) in TestNG?',options:['It delays the test by 5 seconds','It fails the test if it does not complete within 5000 milliseconds','It sets the maximum retry count to 5000','It limits memory usage to 5000 KB'],answer:1,explanation:'timeOut specifies the maximum allowed execution time in milliseconds. If the test exceeds this duration, TestNG interrupts the thread and marks the test FAILED with a timeout message. This prevents hung tests from blocking the suite indefinitely.'},
{id:2240,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What risk does using @Test(timeOut=...) carry in multi-threaded TestNG execution?',options:['No risk; timeOut is always safe','TestNG interrupts the thread using Thread.interrupt(), which may leave shared resources in an inconsistent state if the test does not handle InterruptedException properly','TimeOut only works in single-threaded suites','TimeOut causes the JVM to restart'],answer:1,explanation:'Thread interruption does not forcibly stop all code paths. If the test holds locks, database connections, or file handles, interruption may leak those resources. Robust test design uses try-finally to release resources or checks Thread.isInterrupted() at safe points.'},
{id:2241,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is "dead test" detection in the context of TestNG?',options:['Finding tests that always pass','Identifying test methods that are never executed because of misconfigured groups, dependencies, or missing inclusion in testng.xml','Removing failed tests','Finding tests with no assertions'],answer:1,explanation:'Dead tests are methods annotated with @Test that never run. Common causes include: not being included in any testng.xml <class> list, belonging to an excluded group, or having unsatisfied @Test(dependsOnMethods) chains. Static analysis tools or coverage reports can reveal them.'},
{id:2242,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you detect unused test methods in a large TestNG project?',options:['Manually review every class','Use code coverage tools (JaCoCo) on the test classes themselves, or compare declared @Test methods against testng-results.xml executed methods','TestNG reports unused tests by default','Use @Test(enabled=false) to mark them'],answer:1,explanation:'Running JaCoCo over test execution and checking which @Test methods show 0% invocation indicates unused tests. Alternatively, parsing testng-results.xml and comparing executed method signatures against all @Test-annotated methods in compiled bytecode (using reflection or static analysis) exposes gaps.'},
{id:2243,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does code coverage mean in the context of TestNG and why does high coverage not guarantee test quality?',options:['Coverage measures how many tests pass','Code coverage measures which production code lines are exercised by tests; high coverage does not guarantee quality because tests may exercise code without meaningful assertions','Coverage is only relevant for unit tests','Coverage is measured by number of test methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Code coverage (line, branch, statement) tracks production code executed by tests. A test that calls a method but asserts nothing contributes to coverage without providing safety. Test quality depends on assertion strength and edge-case coverage, not just line execution percentages.'},
{id:2244,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you configure JaCoCo with TestNG in a Maven project to generate a coverage report?',options:['Add @Coverage to test methods','Add the jacoco-maven-plugin with prepare-agent bound to initialize phase and report bound to verify phase; Surefire runs TestNG with the javaagent injected by JaCoCo','Use jacoco { } block in testng.xml','Run mvn jacoco:report separately without configuration'],answer:1,explanation:'The JaCoCo Maven plugin binds prepare-agent to the process-test-classes phase to inject the javaagent JVM arg into Surefire. After tests run, the report goal (verify phase) reads the jacoco.exec data file and generates HTML/XML reports in target/site/jacoco.'},
{id:2245,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a feature toggle (feature flag) in the context of software testing?',options:['A TestNG annotation to skip a test','A runtime switch that enables or disables application features without code deployment; tests use them to verify behaviour under different feature states','A JUnit-specific feature','A TestNG group that can be toggled'],answer:1,explanation:'Feature toggles (flags) allow code paths to be enabled/disabled at runtime via configuration. Tests exercise both the enabled and disabled paths. In TestNG, a @DataProvider or @BeforeMethod can set the flag value before each test, ensuring both states are covered.'},
{id:2246,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you structure a TestNG test that verifies behaviour with a feature flag both on and off?',options:['Write two separate test classes','Use @DataProvider to supply {true, false} as the flag value, configure the feature flag in @BeforeMethod based on the parameter, and assert the expected behaviour for each state','Use @Test(enabled=true) and @Test(enabled=false)','Use two separate testng.xml files'],answer:1,explanation:'A DataProvider returning Object[][]{{true},{false}} parameterises the test. In the test body, the feature flag is set before the action (FeatureFlags.set("myFeature", flagValue)) and the appropriate assertion is applied. Both states are verified in a single test method with two data rows.'},
{id:2247,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is test isolation in TestNG and why is it important?',options:['Running tests in alphabetical order','Ensuring each test is independent of other tests\' execution order, state, and side effects so that any test can run alone or in any order with the same result','Isolating tests to a single thread','Using @Test(singleThreaded=true)'],answer:1,explanation:'Test isolation guarantees reproducible, reliable results. Without it, a test may pass when run alone but fail when run after another test that leaves shared state dirty. Isolation is achieved through proper setup/teardown, avoiding shared mutable state, and using independent data fixtures per test.'},
{id:2248,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What TestNG mechanism most directly prevents test order dependency issues?',options:['@Test(priority=1)','Proper use of @BeforeMethod/@AfterMethod to reset state, combined with avoiding @Test(dependsOnMethods) for unrelated tests','Running tests alphabetically','Using a single-threaded suite'],answer:1,explanation:'@BeforeMethod and @AfterMethod establish a clean, known state before each test and tear down side effects after each test. Avoiding dependsOnMethods for semantically independent tests prevents ordering chains. These practices ensure tests are genuinely order-independent.'},
{id:2249,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does "cross-platform test management" mean for TestNG projects?',options:['Running tests on Linux only','Managing test suites, results, and plans across multiple operating systems, CI platforms, and environments; TestNG\'s XML-based configuration and standard result formats facilitate this','Using TestNG on mobile only','Sharing testng.xml across team members'],answer:1,explanation:'Cross-platform management involves running the same TestNG suite on Windows, Linux, and macOS CI agents; sharing testng.xml in version control; and publishing results to a central test management tool (Xray, Zephyr, ALM). TestNG\'s OS-neutral configuration makes this straightforward.'},
{id:2250,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of the @Test(enabled=false) attribute?',options:['Marks the test as failing','Disables the test method so TestNG skips it without executing it','Marks the test for deletion','Runs the test in a separate JVM'],answer:1,explanation:'@Test(enabled=false) prevents TestNG from executing the annotated method. It is reported as a disabled test but does not appear as SKIP in most reports. This is a quick way to temporarily exclude a broken test without deleting it or removing its annotation.'},
{id:2251,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does TestNG handle a @Test method whose @BeforeMethod configuration method throws an exception?',options:['The test still runs normally','The test is marked as FAIL with a configuration failure; the @AfterMethod still runs if configured with alwaysRun=true','The test is skipped silently','The suite aborts immediately'],answer:1,explanation:'A configuration failure in @BeforeMethod causes dependent @Test methods to be marked FAIL (configuration failure). To ensure cleanup still happens, @AfterMethod should be annotated with alwaysRun=true, which ensures it runs even when the preceding configuration method failed.'},
{id:2252,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the significance of @BeforeMethod(alwaysRun=true) in TestNG?',options:['The method always runs before every test regardless of group filters','The method runs even if the test is in a group that is excluded by the current configuration\'s group filters','The method runs even after failures, same as @AfterMethod(alwaysRun=true)','alwaysRun has no effect on @BeforeMethod'],answer:1,explanation:'alwaysRun=true on @BeforeMethod ensures the setup runs even for test methods that belong to groups not in the current group filter. Without it, if a test is not selected by the group filter, its @BeforeMethod may be skipped too, which can cause unexpected state.'},
{id:2253,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @Test(singleThreaded=true) do in TestNG?',options:['Forces all tests in the class to run in a single thread sequentially even when the suite uses parallel="methods"','Makes the test run faster','Prevents other tests from starting until this one finishes','It is equivalent to @Test(sequential=true) in JUnit'],answer:0,explanation:'When parallel="methods" is set, TestNG may dispatch different methods of the same class to different threads. @Test(singleThreaded=true) at the class level forces all methods in that class to run on the same thread, preventing concurrency issues for classes that use shared mutable instance state.'},
{id:2254,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What TestNG feature allows grouping related test methods so that a failure in one skips the rest in the group?',options:['@Test(groups="myGroup") with <dependencies> in testng.xml','@Test(dependsOnGroups="setup") combined with the group dependency mechanism','@Test(skipOnFail=true)','@BeforeGroup(failFast=true)'],answer:1,explanation:'@Test(dependsOnGroups={"setup"}) makes the test depend on all tests in the "setup" group. If any test in "setup" fails, dependent tests are SKIPPED rather than FAILED, enabling fast-fail behaviour for logically chained test flows.'},
{id:2255,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you run a specific test class from the command line using the TestNG CLI?',options:['java org.testng.TestNG -testclass com.example.MyTest','testng --class com.example.MyTest','mvn test -Dclass=com.example.MyTest','gradle test --testclass com.example.MyTest'],answer:0,explanation:'The TestNG CLI accepts -testclass (or -testClass) followed by the fully qualified class name to run a specific class without a testng.xml file. For example: java -cp ... org.testng.TestNG -testclass com.example.MyTest.'},
{id:2256,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What attribute in @Test specifies the maximum number of milliseconds a test is allowed to run?',options:['maxTime','timeout','timeOut','duration'],answer:2,explanation:'The attribute is timeOut (camelCase). Example: @Test(timeOut=3000). TestNG uses a separate thread and interrupts it if the execution exceeds the specified millisecond value, marking the test as failed with a timeout error.'},
{id:2257,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you configure TestNG to fail the entire suite if the overall pass rate drops below 80%?',options:['Set pass-rate="80" in testng.xml','Implement ISuiteListener.onFinish() to compute pass rate from ISuite.getResults() and throw an exception or call System.exit()','Use @Suite(minPassRate=80)','TestNG does not support pass rate thresholds'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG has no built-in pass-rate threshold. A custom ISuiteListener computes the rate in onFinish() using ISuite.getResults(), iterating ITestContext results. If rate < threshold, you can throw a RuntimeException or call System.exit(1) to fail the build.'},
{id:2258,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the role of the ITestContext in TestNG?',options:['It is the object that holds test configuration, result sets (passed/failed/skipped), and attributes shared within a single <test> tag execution','It manages the WebDriver instance','It is the root context for the entire suite','It is an alias for ISuite'],answer:0,explanation:'ITestContext is provided to @BeforeTest, @AfterTest methods and listeners. It gives access to the test\'s configuration (ITestNGMethod list), result sets for passed/failed/skipped tests, and a String-keyed attribute map for sharing data across methods within the same <test> block.'},
{id:2259,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you share data between @BeforeClass and @Test methods in TestNG without using static fields?',options:['Use @Test(dataProvider=...) only','Store data in ITestContext attributes via context.setAttribute("key", value) in @BeforeClass and retrieve via context.getAttribute("key") in @Test','Use a public field','TestNG does not allow sharing data between lifecycle methods'],answer:1,explanation:'ITestContext.setAttribute() stores arbitrary data within the current <test> scope. @BeforeClass and @Test methods can both accept ITestContext as a parameter, enabling data sharing without static fields, which is essential for thread-safe parallel test execution.'},
{id:2260,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the preserve-order="true" attribute on a <test> element in testng.xml do?',options:['Runs tests in reverse order','Runs @Test methods in the order they are declared in the <test> block, preserving testng.xml class and method declaration order','Sorts methods alphabetically','preserve-order has no effect'],answer:1,explanation:'By default TestNG may reorder tests for efficiency. preserve-order="true" runs classes and methods in the order they appear in the testng.xml file. This is useful when integration tests have a natural sequence that must be maintained.'},
{id:2261,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you exclude a specific test method from a group in testng.xml?',options:['@Test(groups="!smoke") on the method','<exclude name="methodName"/> inside the <methods> element of the <class> definition in testng.xml','@Test(excludeFromGroup="smoke")','You cannot exclude individual methods; only classes'],answer:1,explanation:'Within a <class> element in testng.xml, a <methods> block can list <exclude name="methodName"/> entries. This overrides the class-level inclusion, preventing specific methods from running while the rest of the class executes normally.'},
{id:2262,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of @Listeners({MyListener.class}) annotation on a TestNG test class?',options:['Imports the listener class','Registers the listener for only that test class, so it receives events only from that class\'s tests','Makes the listener a global suite listener','@Listeners is invalid; listeners must be in testng.xml'],answer:1,explanation:'@Listeners({}) on a class registers the specified listener(s) for the duration of that class\'s execution. It is scoped to the class, unlike testng.xml listeners which apply globally. This allows class-specific behaviours like custom screenshots only for certain test classes.'},
{id:2263,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which TestNG interface would you implement to transform annotations at runtime, for example to dynamically set timeOut based on an environment variable?',options:['IAnnotationTransformer','IAnnotationTransformer2','ITestListener','IMethodInterceptor'],answer:0,explanation:'IAnnotationTransformer.transform() is called for every @Test method before execution. You receive the ITestAnnotation object and can modify attributes like timeOut, retryAnalyzer, groups, or enabled. This allows annotation values to be configured from external sources without modifying source code.'},
{id:2264,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is IMethodInterceptor in TestNG?',options:['Intercepts exceptions thrown by test methods','Intercepts HTTP calls made during tests','Allows reordering or filtering the list of test methods to run at suite start; its intercept() method returns the modified list','Intercepts annotation processing'],answer:2,explanation:'IMethodInterceptor.intercept() receives the full list of IMethodInstance objects before execution begins. You can reorder, filter, or add methods dynamically. This is a powerful extension point for custom ordering strategies, dynamic group filtering, or adaptive test selection.'},
{id:2265,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does TestNG\'s dependency injection work for test method parameters without @Parameters?',options:['TestNG cannot inject parameters without @Parameters','TestNG injects known types like ITestContext, ITestResult, XmlTest, and Method automatically based on parameter type, no annotation required','Only Spring injection works','Use @Autowired'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG\'s internal injection resolves certain types by type-matching parameter declarations in @Test, @Before*, @After* methods. ITestContext, ITestResult, XmlTest, and java.lang.reflect.Method are injected automatically without any annotation, reducing coupling to the TestNG API.'},
{id:2266,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the default behaviour of TestNG when a @Test method does not specify any groups?',options:['It is excluded from all runs','It belongs to the default group and runs unless explicitly excluded','It is marked as pending','It always runs last'],answer:1,explanation:'Test methods without an explicit groups attribute belong to a default unnamed group. They are included in suite runs unless specifically excluded. This makes the transition from no-group to group-based organisation gradual and backward-compatible.'},
{id:2267,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the effect of @Test(invocationCount=5) in TestNG?',options:['Runs the test with 5 different data sets','Runs the test method 5 times sequentially (or in parallel if threadPoolSize is set)','Creates 5 instances of the test class','Retries the test up to 5 times on failure'],answer:1,explanation:'invocationCount runs the test method the specified number of times. Combined with threadPoolSize=5, the 5 invocations run in parallel threads. This is useful for load testing a component or verifying that a test is not flaky across repeated executions.'},
{id:2268,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does @Test(invocationCount=N, successPercentage=P) work together in TestNG?',options:['successPercentage sets the number of passing invocations required','The test passes if at least P% of N invocations succeed; useful for stability tests where occasional failures are acceptable','successPercentage retries failed invocations','They cannot be used together'],answer:1,explanation:'successPercentage (0–100) sets the minimum pass rate required across all invocations. With invocationCount=10 and successPercentage=80, the test passes if at least 8 of 10 invocations pass. This explicitly acknowledges and quantifies acceptable flakiness in stability testing scenarios.'},
{id:2269,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happens to tests that @Test(dependsOnMethods={"setUp"}) depends on when setUp is marked @Test(enabled=false)?',options:['Dependent tests run normally','Dependent tests are SKIPPED because the dependency is considered unavailable','Dependent tests FAIL','TestNG ignores the dependency'],answer:1,explanation:'If a dependency method is disabled, TestNG considers it absent. Dependent tests are SKIPPED because their prerequisite never ran. This propagates the skip transitively through the dependency chain until a test with no dependency on the disabled method is reached.'},
{id:2270,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the TestNG concept of "hard assert" versus "soft assert"?',options:['Hard asserts use Assert; soft asserts use SoftAssert — hard asserts stop test on failure, soft asserts collect all failures and report at assertAll()','Hard asserts are faster','Soft asserts require a license','There is no distinction; both are the same'],answer:0,explanation:'Hard asserts (org.testng.Assert) throw AssertionError immediately on failure, aborting the test. Soft asserts (org.testng.asserts.SoftAssert) collect failures and throw a combined error only when assertAll() is called. Soft asserts enable full test completion for diagnostic purposes.'},
{id:2271,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the correct way to use SoftAssert in a @DataProvider-driven TestNG test?',options:['Share one SoftAssert instance across all data rows','Create a new SoftAssert instance per test invocation (in the test method body or via @BeforeMethod) and call assertAll() at the end of each test invocation','Call assertAll() only in @AfterSuite','SoftAssert cannot be used with DataProviders'],answer:1,explanation:'Each data row triggers a separate test method invocation. Creating a new SoftAssert per invocation ensures failures from one row do not contaminate the next. The simplest approach is declaring SoftAssert sa = new SoftAssert() inside the test method and calling sa.assertAll() at the end.'},
{id:2272,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which TestNG configuration allows specifying the output directory for test reports?',options:['<suite output-dir="custom-reports">','Set -d path/to/dir on the TestNG CLI, or outputDirectory in Maven Surefire plugin configuration','@Suite(outputDir="custom-reports")','Reports cannot be redirected in TestNG'],answer:1,explanation:'The -d flag on the TestNG CLI sets the output directory. In Maven Surefire, use <reportsDirectory> or pass -Dsurefire.reportNameSuffix. In Gradle, configure reports.junitXml.destination. The default test-output directory can be overridden for CI artifact collection.'},
{id:2273,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of the verbose attribute in a TestNG testng.xml suite declaration?',options:['Sets test method priority','Controls the level of console output; higher values (0–10) produce more detailed logging during test execution','Enables verbose assertion messages','Verbose is a deprecated attribute'],answer:1,explanation:'<suite verbose="2"> controls TestNG\'s console verbosity. Level 0 shows nothing; level 10 shows all internal TestNG events including method dispatch, listener invocations, and thread assignments. Higher verbosity is invaluable for debugging configuration issues.'},
{id:2274,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure a TestNG suite to stop execution after the first test failure?',options:['<suite haltOnFailure="true">','<suite configfailurepolicy="skip"> with a custom listener that calls System.exit on first failure','There is no built-in haltOnFailure; implement ISuiteListener or ITestListener.onTestFailure to throw an exception','@Suite(failFast=true)'],answer:2,explanation:'TestNG does not have a native haltOnFailure attribute (unlike some other runners). The standard workaround is implementing ITestListener.onTestFailure() to throw a RuntimeException or call System.exit(1), which aborts the JVM. Some community extensions also provide this capability.'},
{id:2275,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the configfailurepolicy attribute in TestNG testng.xml and what values does it accept?',options:['It controls whether @Test failures skip subsequent tests; values: skip/continue','It controls what happens when a configuration method (@Before*/@After*) fails; values: "skip" (default — skips dependent tests) or "continue" (runs dependent tests anyway)','It is not a real attribute','It accepts: abort, skip, continue, retry'],answer:1,explanation:'configfailurepolicy determines behaviour after @BeforeMethod/@BeforeClass failures. "skip" (default) marks dependent @Test methods as SKIPPED. "continue" runs them anyway despite the configuration failure. "continue" is risky but useful when you want to see all failures even after setup issues.'},
{id:2276,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @Test(groups={"functional","regression"}) in TestNG?',options:['Runs the test twice, once per group','Assigns the test to multiple groups simultaneously so it can be selected by either group filter','Creates a group dependency','Groups cannot be combined in one annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'A test method can belong to multiple groups by listing them in the groups array. This enables flexible selection: running -groups functional picks all functional tests including this one, and -groups regression also picks it. Multi-group membership models orthogonal classification dimensions.'},
{id:2277,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @BeforeGroups({"smoke"}) do in TestNG?',options:['Runs before every test in the "smoke" group — once before the first test in that group runs in the current <test> block','Runs before every individual test method','Replaces @BeforeClass for group members','Creates the "smoke" group'],answer:0,explanation:'@BeforeGroups({"smoke"}) executes once before the first test belonging to the "smoke" group in the current execution context. It is used for group-level setup (e.g., starting a service needed only by smoke tests) rather than class-level or method-level setup.'},
{id:2278,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a potential issue when using @BeforeGroups in a parallel TestNG suite?',options:['@BeforeGroups is not thread-safe','No issue; @BeforeGroups is always safe in parallel suites','If multiple test methods in the group start simultaneously, @BeforeGroups may not have completed before all of them begin, depending on the parallel mode and thread-count','@BeforeGroups is not supported in parallel mode'],answer:2,explanation:'@BeforeGroups is intended to run once before the first method in the group. In highly parallel configurations, TestNG\'s guarantee about exactly-once execution and completion before other group members start can be unreliable. Consider using @BeforeClass or suite-level setup for resources shared across parallel group members.'},
{id:2279,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you run TestNG tests matching a specific regular expression from the Maven command line?',options:['-Dtest=*Smoke*','-Dit.test=.*Smoke.*','-Dgroups=smoke','mvn test -Dtest=".*Smoke.*" — Maven Surefire passes this as a class name pattern to TestNG'],answer:3,explanation:'Maven Surefire\'s -Dtest parameter accepts patterns. For TestNG, it is interpreted as a class name pattern. -Dtest=".*SmokeTest" runs all classes matching that pattern. Group-based filtering via -Dgroups is more idiomatic for TestNG\'s group-based organisation.'},
{id:2280,topic:'testng',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does the @AfterSuite annotation guarantee in TestNG?',options:['It runs after every test method','It runs exactly once after all tests in the entire suite have finished, regardless of pass/fail outcomes','It runs after every class','It runs after each group'],answer:1,explanation:'@AfterSuite is the last lifecycle method to execute in a TestNG run. It is ideal for global cleanup like shutting down containers, closing database connection pools, or generating final summary reports that require all test results to be available.'},
{id:2281,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between @AfterTest and @AfterSuite in TestNG?',options:['They are identical','@AfterTest runs after all tests in a <test> XML block; @AfterSuite runs after all tests in the entire <suite>','@AfterTest runs after each test method; @AfterSuite runs after each class','@AfterTest is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'A testng.xml <suite> can contain multiple <test> blocks. @AfterTest runs after all methods in one <test> block complete. @AfterSuite runs once after the entire <suite> finishes. This distinction allows different levels of teardown granularity.'},
{id:2282,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you implement test isolation for tests that modify a shared singleton service in TestNG?',options:['Use @Test(sequential=true)','In @BeforeMethod, take a snapshot of the singleton\'s state; in @AfterMethod, restore the snapshot, or use dependency injection with a fresh mock per test','Use @Test(isolated=true)','Singletons cannot be tested with TestNG'],answer:1,explanation:'For singletons, either: (1) use a spy/mock to intercept and reset state, (2) expose a reset() method called in @AfterMethod, or (3) replace the singleton with a fresh instance via reflection or a factory in @BeforeMethod. This ensures each test starts with a known state.'},
{id:2283,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the ITestResult object passed to ITestListener methods?',options:['It modifies the test result','It provides read-only information about the test outcome including method name, parameters, start/end time, throwable, and status constants','It is used to retry the test','It represents the test class instance'],answer:1,explanation:'ITestResult encapsulates the complete outcome of a test method invocation. Status constants (ITestResult.SUCCESS, FAILURE, SKIP) identify the outcome. getThrowable() retrieves the failure cause. getParameters() gives the data provider arguments used for that invocation.'},
{id:2284,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you attach a screenshot to the TestNG HTML report on test failure?',options:['@Test(screenshot=true)','In ITestListener.onTestFailure(), capture the screenshot and write it to the test-output directory, then use Reporter.log() with an HTML img tag referencing the file path','TestNG automatically captures screenshots','Use @AfterMethod only'],answer:1,explanation:'The standard pattern: ITestListener.onTestFailure() calls WebDriver.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE), saves the file to test-output/screenshots/, then calls Reporter.log("<img src=\\"screenshots/name.png\\">") to embed an HTML image link in the per-test section of the report.'},
{id:2285,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does TestNG\'s parallel="classes" mode differ from parallel="methods" for class-level state management?',options:['They are identical','parallel="classes" runs each class in its own thread but all methods of a class in the same thread; parallel="methods" may dispatch different methods of the same class to different threads','parallel="classes" is faster','parallel="methods" is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'parallel="classes" assigns one thread per class, so instance state is accessed only by one thread at a time — safe for classes with shared mutable fields. parallel="methods" may run different methods of the same class concurrently, requiring thread-safe design or @Test(singleThreaded=true).'},
{id:2286,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the use of the @Test(priority=N) attribute in TestNG?',options:['Sets thread priority','Determines execution order within a class — lower priority values run first; default is 0','Sets test weight for reporting','Marks tests as high-importance'],answer:1,explanation:'Priority is an integer where lower values run first. @Test(priority=-1) runs before @Test(priority=0). Methods with the same priority run in an unspecified order. Priority affects intra-class ordering only and does not create formal dependencies (unlike dependsOnMethods).'},
{id:2287,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Why is using @Test(priority=N) for test ordering considered an antipattern in most TestNG guidance?',options:['Priority values are hard to remember','Priority creates implicit ordering without enforcing the actual dependency; if an earlier test fails, later tests still run and may fail due to missing state rather than being skipped','Priority is too slow','It is not an antipattern'],answer:1,explanation:'@Test(dependsOnMethods) explicitly models the relationship and causes dependent tests to skip if dependencies fail. Priority just orders execution without the skip-on-failure behaviour. Tests ordered by priority are also fragile: adding a new test may require renumbering all priorities.'},
{id:2288,topic:'testng',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Extent Reports library and how is it used with TestNG?',options:['It is a TestNG built-in reporter','ExtentReports (by Extent Framework) is a third-party reporting library integrated with TestNG via an ITestListener or IReporter implementation that produces rich HTML dashboards with charts and logs','It replaces testng.xml','It is a Gradle plugin'],answer:1,explanation:'ExtentReports provides visually rich HTML reports with timelines, pie charts, and detailed logs. Integration is via ExtentTestNGITestListener or a custom IReporter. The listener creates ExtentTest instances per method, logs steps, and flushes the report in onFinish().'},
{id:2289,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you ensure TestNG configuration methods are executed in a deterministic order when multiple @BeforeMethod methods exist in the same class?',options:['Alphabetical order is always used','Combine all @BeforeMethod logic into a single method; TestNG does not guarantee order among multiple @BeforeMethod methods in the same class','Use @BeforeMethod(order=1)','Priority attribute applies to @BeforeMethod'],answer:1,explanation:'TestNG does not guarantee execution order for multiple @BeforeMethod annotations in the same class. The reliable approach is to consolidate them into a single @BeforeMethod that calls helper methods in the desired sequence, giving full explicit control over setup order.'},
{id:2290,topic:'testng',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What strategy prevents TestNG tests from interfering with each other when using a shared WireMock server instance across parallel tests?',options:['Use a single global stub for all tests','Start a separate WireMock server instance per test class (or per thread) using random ports, register stubs in @BeforeMethod, and reset stubs in @AfterMethod using WireMock.resetAllRequests() and WireMock.resetAllScenarios()','Parallel tests cannot use WireMock','Use a static port for all tests'],answer:1,explanation:'A shared WireMock server with static stubs causes parallel tests to interfere because stubs and request journals are shared. Using per-class instances on random ports (WireMockServer(0)) eliminates port conflicts. Per-test stub registration in @BeforeMethod with resetAllRequests() in @AfterMethod ensures each test gets a clean HTTP mock environment.'}
]);
